          "Without short bounded Time-to-Live expiration or distributed invalidation hooks, caching introduces dangerous authorization lag—allowing a recently fired employee or revoked API client to keep accessing sensitive documents until stale cache entries finally age out. Adversaries deliberately blast rapid bursts right after permission changes hoping stale edge caches grant lingering unauthorized reads.",
          "`HighThroughputPolicyCache` holds `O(1)` lookup and insertion complexity while pairing bounded LRU eviction with short 30-second TTLs and pub/sub invalidation listeners. Even massive API clusters sustain sub-millisecond P99 gateway latency under heavy spike loads without blowing worker memory footprints or hitting database bottlenecks on shared ReBAC directories.",
          "Cache engineering edge cases cover tuple key collisions if tenants omit explicit namespace prefixes, stale negative caches locking out newly promoted users briefly, and cache stampedes when popular shared keys expire simultaneously under high concurrency. Keying cache maps strictly by full `(tenant, user, action, resource)` tuples and using request coalescing prevents cross-tenant leakage and backend stampedes.",
