          "To establish true defense-in-depth, modern container platforms enforce four foundational security pillars: rootless users, capability drops, seccomp filters, and read-only root filesystems.",
          "Linux capabilities divide traditional monolithic root privileges into distinct units; dropping `CAP_SYS_ADMIN` and `CAP_NET_RAW` eliminates kernel module loading and raw packet sniffing.",
          "Seccomp-BPF (Secure Computing with Berkeley Packet Filters) intercepts system calls made by container processes, blocking dangerous calls like `ptrace` and `mount`.",
          "Inside `CONTAINER_SECURITY_PILLARS`, four core runtime controls govern Linux container defense in depth: dropping excess POSIX kernel capabilities, filtering dangerous system calls via `seccomp-BPF`, running non-zero unprivileged UIDs (rootless execution), and locking root filesystems read-only. Because every container on a Kubernetes worker node shares one underlying Linux kernel, restricting process privileges prevents single compromised containers from breaking out onto host nodes.",
